<speak>
<s>From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">15032026</say-as>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>Former CIA director David <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="pəˈtreɪəs">Petraeus</phoneme> this week became the first senior American official to say publicly what the war's critics had argued: the United States struck Iran not in self-defence, but to destroy its air defences so Israel could operate freely.</s> <s>Turkey's foreign minister <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈhɑːkɑːn fiˈdɑːn">Hakan Fidan</phoneme> added that Washington launched those strikes while nuclear negotiations with Tehran were still active, and that Iran feels betrayed — a repeated pattern he said will make future talks structurally harder to revive.</s> <s>Washington has still not stated what conditions would end the war.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>The Israeli military threatened to bomb Lebanese ambulances this week, citing potential military use as justification — a legal threshold under the Geneva Conventions that requires proof Israel has not provided publicly.</s> <s>Twenty-six medical workers have been killed in Lebanon since the second of March.</s> <s>Lebanon's health ministry has not said whether it will halt ambulance operations to protect remaining crews.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Israeli and American strikes have severely damaged Iran's Space Research Centre in Tehran — the country's primary satellite and intelligence-mapping facility.</s> <s>No government has designated it a military installation, and Iran has brought that distinction to the United Nations.</s> <s>Tehran has given no indication whether it will attempt to rebuild its space programme while the strikes continue.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>India holds the chair of the BRICS economic bloc this year, yet has issued no statement as a fellow member, Iran, is bombed.</s> <s>When Brazil chaired in 2025, the group condemned equivalent strikes twice; the contrast has not gone unnoticed in Tehran, which has urged India to activate the bloc.</s> <s>India's foreign minister <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="dʒaɪˈʃæŋkər">Jaishankar</phoneme> confirmed a fourth call with Tehran since late February but made no commitment on collective action.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Japan has deployed the Type-12 missile — a domestically built system with a range of one thousand kilometres, five times its predecessor — giving Japan the ability to strike Chinese naval assets for the first time.</s> <s>The deployment arrives as American forces are consumed by the Iran war, and analysts in Beijing have called it a strategic threshold crossed.</s> <s>Prime Minister <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="tɑːˈkɑːiːtʃi">Takaichi</phoneme> travels to Washington this week under US pressure to increase defence spending further still.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Saudi Arabia downed seven drones over Riyadh, activating Patriot missile batteries that Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman has installed around the capital he is positioning as a post-oil global financial centre.</s> <s>The defence ministry named no attacker.</s> <s>Russia is simultaneously shipping Shahed drones back to Iran — the same model Iran originally designed and supplied to Moscow for use in Ukraine — binding two wars in a single supply chain, so that each drone reaching Tehran is one not fired at Kyiv.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>A KC-135 aerial refuelling tanker crashed in Iraq on Thursday, killing six American airmen and bringing confirmed US war deaths to thirteen.</s> <s>The sixty-year-old aircraft has been flown at elevated intensity during the Iran campaign, straining airframes designed for Cold War peacetime operations, not sustained combat sorties.</s> <s>An Iran-backed Iraqi militia claimed responsibility for the crash.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Pakistan raised the price of kerosene by ninety percent while holding petrol prices steady, directing its twenty-three-billion-rupee fuel subsidy toward urban drivers rather than the rural households who depend on kerosene for heat.</s> <s>Separately, the Hormuz blockade has doubled freight rates and added war-risk insurance premiums that have made commodity imports effectively unviable; some textile producers now hold just fifteen days of raw materials.</s> <s>Industry groups forecast a ten to twenty percent drop in textile exports for March.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>The mechanism linking the Hormuz closure to rising electricity bills worldwide is a market rule: power grids price all electricity at the rate of the most expensive source dispatched, which is almost always gas, meaning solar and wind power costs climb alongside it.</s> <s>Japan, which rebuilt its gas dependency after Fukushima and routes seventy percent of its crude through the strait, faces some of the sharpest exposure.</s> <s>Countries with abundant renewables are not insulated — gas sets the price regardless of how little of it they actually burn.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>In Thailand, farmers are selling coconuts for six United States cents each — a ninety percent collapse from pandemic-era highs.</s> <s>Chinese investors financed wholesale buyers who now control the supply chain, capturing the margin that once reached growers.</s> <s>Thailand has set no floor price, and an eighty-one-year-old farmer and his wife cannot afford to retire from the orchards they have worked their whole lives.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>American and Chinese economic envoys opened trade talks in Paris this week, but the surrounding context makes a quick deal distant.</s> <s>China buys most of Iran's oil and has supplied it with weapons, giving Beijing simultaneous leverage over the Iran war and the American economy at the same table.</s> <s>Analysts see little prospect for an agreement before a planned summit between the two leaders, God willing, in Beijing.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Every living cell contains thousands of nuclear pore complexes — the gatekeepers controlling all molecular traffic in and out of the nucleus — and for decades the disordered proteins at their centre were dismissed as structural noise.</s> <s>New research shows that disorder is the mechanism itself: the apparent chaos performs the selective filtering that determines what reaches the cell's command centre.</s> <s>The discovery may reshape how drugs are engineered to cross into the nucleus.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Boston Dynamics' Atlas robot still cannot reliably climb stairs in 2026.</s> <s>Former company researcher Scott <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈkaɪndərzmɑː">Kuindersma</phoneme> says variable terrain — uneven steps, unfamiliar surfaces — remains unsolved despite rapid advances in large language models, motors, and computer vision.</s> <s>If Atlas cannot navigate a staircase, Tesla's timeline to deploy its Optimus humanoid robot in homes deserves considerably more scepticism than it tends to receive.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.</s>
</speak>